---
title: Dimensions
summary: Run one site across a matrix of versions or locales. Each axis prefixes the slug, the outline is built per coordinate, and an untranslated page falls back to the default instead of answering 404.
related:
  - content/outline
  - content/site
  - content/collections
---

Docs for `v1` and `v2`, a site in `en` and `de`, or a stable and a next channel usually share one structure: the same page exists in several editions. Building a second site for each edition duplicates the navigation, the search and every link. `dimensions()` treats a version or a locale as a **coordinate** on the same outline instead. It wraps N outlines (one per coordinate) into a single `Outline` that `site()` consumes, and parses the coordinate off the front of the slug.

If your versions are genuinely different content, they belong in different collections, and you don't need this page; dimensions are for one structure read at several coordinates.

## An axis over collections

You declare the axes, plus a `resolve` function that returns the outline for a coordinate. Versions are ordinary collections, so `resolve` returns whichever collection holds each one:

::: code-group

```ts [one axis — versions]
import { site, dimensions } from '@ogygia/content';
import { v1, v2 } from './collections.server';

export const docs = site({
	base: '/docs',
	outline: dimensions({
		axes: { version: { values: ['v2', 'v1'], default: 'v2', label: 'Version' } },
		resolve: ({ version }) => (version === 'v1' ? v1 : v2)
	})
});
// /docs/routing        → v2 (the default serves bare)
// /docs/v1/routing     → v1
```

```ts [two axes — version × locale]
export const docs = site({
	base: '/docs',
	outline: dimensions({
		axes: {
			version: { values: ['v2', 'v1'], default: 'v2', label: 'Version' },
			locale: { values: ['en', 'de'], default: 'en', label: 'Language', fallback: true }
		},
		resolve: ({ version, locale }) => corpora[version][locale]
	})
});
// /docs/routing        → v2 × en
// /docs/de/routing     → v2 × de
// /docs/v1/de/routing  → v1 × de — the axes compose
```

:::

The **default** coordinate is served at the bare URL and the others are prefixed. `resolve` is called once per distinct coordinate and the result is memoized, so a version's outline is built lazily on its first visit.

## Fallback, not 404

Missing translations are the most common failure on versioned docs sites. With dimensions, an untranslated page **renders the default coordinate's content at its own URL** instead of answering 404. Because every address is guaranteed to resolve, the switcher can always link the same slug across coordinates.

`docs.page()` reports what happened in its `fallback` field, so the page can show a "not yet translated" note:

```svelte
<script>
	const view = await docs.page(page.params.slug);
	// view.coordinate → { locale: 'de' };  view.fallback → { axis: 'locale', from: 'de', to: 'en' }
	// fallback is null when the page is native to its coordinate
</script>

{#if view.fallback}
	<aside>This page isn't translated to {view.fallback.from} yet — showing the {view.fallback.to} original.</aside>
{/if}
```

Fallback is **opt-in per axis** (`fallback: true` on the axis). A locale usually wants it, but a version usually does not, because a page missing from `v1` most often means it _did not exist yet_, and that should be a 404.

## The switcher

`docs.switcher(slug)` returns the version/locale picker for the coordinate in `slug`, with its hrefs already filled in. It is a serializable value (one `SwitcherAxis` per axis) that the `<Switcher>` component renders as a dropdown. Switching keeps you on the _same page_, landing on its equivalent in the chosen coordinate (or in the default, by the fallback rule).

You will rarely call it directly, because `docs.meta(slug)` bundles `{ nav, switcher, data }` in one call and `<DocsShell {meta}>` renders the switcher whenever it is non-null.

## Nav and search across the matrix

- **`nav(slug)`**: pass the slug so that the tree reflects that coordinate; the [remote](/docs/content/remotes) passes it through for you. A bare coordinate prefix such as `'v1/'` works too.
- **`docs.entries()`**: includes the **union** of addresses across the matrix, so a page that exists in only one coordinate still prerenders there.
- **search**: indexes only the canonical addresses of the _default_ coordinate, so fallback pages under other coordinates don't show up as duplicate hits.

## Coordinate as data

The coordinate is derived from the slug and passed along as data, never kept in module-level state, which makes it safe under concurrent SSR requests. You never need to reach for it yourself: `nav`, `page`, `switcher` and `entries` all take the slug and derive it.

## Do / don't

- **Do** model versions and locales as axes on one outline, which keeps one structure instead of N parallel sites.
- **Do** rely on the fallback for locales, so that a missing translation degrades to the default rather than breaking the URL.
- **Don't** keep the coordinate in module state. Pass the slug, from which the coordinate is derived purely and safely per request.
- **Don't** index every coordinate for search. The default's canonical set is the accurate result, since fallbacks are the same page.
